# Scalpel rules
#
# Every *.rules file in this folder is loaded. Split your rules across as many files as you like.
# Everything below is commented out, so this file does nothing until you change it.
#
# One rule per line:   <verb> <type> <pattern> [option]
#
# Verbs
#   keep     protects matching ids from every other rule
#   hide     the content stays and works as normal, but JEI, EMI and creative tabs leave it out
#   redact   swaps the content for a blank "Redacted" placeholder (the safe choice)
#   remove   the content never loads at all (saves the most, but some mods crash without it)
#   If several rules match the same id, the gentler one wins: keep, then hide, then redact, then remove.
#
# Types
#   item, block, entity, any (all three), recipe, loot, advancement, tag
#   Recipes, loot tables, advancements and tags can only be kept or removed.
#   Only items, blocks and entities can be hidden or redacted.
#
# Patterns
#   examplemod:ruby            one exact id
#   examplemod:*               everything from one mod
#   examplemod:*_eggs          * matches any run of characters, ? matches one
#   *:*_slab                   every slab from every mod
#   /^examplemod:[a-z_]*_eggs$/   a regular expression, between slashes
#
# Options (redact/remove rules for items and blocks only)
#   drop       recipes that use this content are removed (the default, see scalpel.toml)
#   rewrite    recipes keep working without this ingredient where that still makes sense
#
# Examples
#
# redact any    examplemod:*
# keep   block  examplemod:ruby_block
# hide   item   examplemod:creative_*
# remove item   examplemod:*_eggs        rewrite
# remove recipe minecraft:*_boat
# remove loot   minecraft:chests/*
# remove tag    forge:ores/tin
#
# Try a pattern in game before restarting:   /scalpel test item examplemod:*_eggs
# Set dryRun = true in scalpel.toml to see what would be cut without cutting anything.
